00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.270
but also a little bit tired.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:50.080
And so I'll try
to keep it lively.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:53.020
So what I want to do today is
sort of step back a little.

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:56.290
We've been talking a
lot about open data,

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:59.714
but I think we tend to kind
of focus on that upper level

00:01:59.714 --> 00:02:01.630
and say, oh, there's all
these great things we

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.040
can do with open data.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.540
But when you're an
active researcher

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:07.270
and you actually decide
to do this yourself,

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:08.740
there's that moment
of panic where

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:11.230
you say, where do I start?

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.340
I don't know how to
actually do this.

00:02:13.340 --> 00:02:15.610
So yes, I'd love
to share my data,

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:19.180
but what are the steps involved
in actually doing that?

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.010
So I'm going to back
up a little bit,

00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:23.860
talk about what do
we mean by open data,

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:26.980
and walk you through a little
bit of my own experience

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:29.770
in sharing my data personally.

00:02:29.770 --> 00:02:32.440
So just to start off.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:34.420
So this-- the presentation
is openly licensed,

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.610
so feel free to
take photos, tweet,

00:02:36.610 --> 00:02:38.320
do whatever you like with it.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:40.420
After the talk, I'll
post it on figshare

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:45.710
so you can have those slides
to reuse however you'd like.

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:47.380
So I just wanted to
start off with kind

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:50.590
of a description of what
we mean by open science.

00:02:50.590 --> 00:02:53.620
So open data is really
just one small part

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:57.070
of this bigger picture that is
open research and open science.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:59.620
So as researchers,
we're producing

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.180
all kinds of different outputs.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.280
We're producing articles,
but those articles

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.000
are just the very
tiny end product

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.200
of a much bigger process,
this research lifecycle

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.590
that Brian was talking about.

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.690
So when we talk
about open science,

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:16.000
yes, we have open
data, but we also

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.190
have open notebooks, which
I'll talk a little bit

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:21.250
about, open source,
so sharing your code,

00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:23.740
and all kinds of other
aspects, open peer review,

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:25.690
open educational resources.

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:29.817
With the idea that as
educators and as researchers,

00:03:29.817 --> 00:03:31.900
we should be sharing all
these products that we're

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:33.340
producing along the way.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.007
So I'm only going to focus on
open data and open notebooks

00:03:37.007 --> 00:03:38.590
and open source
today, but just wanted

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:42.220
to give you the idea that it's
a much bigger space than just

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:43.520
what we're seeing today.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:45.250
So the Foster Group
has actually worked

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:48.790
out what they call an
open science taxonomy,

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:51.290
which I think is fantastic.

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:54.400
And so you can see some
of the main categories

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.730
they have here, open access,
open data, open science

00:03:57.730 --> 00:03:59.440
evaluation, policies, tools.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:00.940
And we're really
just going to focus

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:02.273
on the ones I have circled here.

00:04:02.273 --> 00:04:03.980
So what do we mean by open data?

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:06.040
What are the
international standards

00:04:06.040 --> 00:04:08.230
for that, for data
use and reuse?

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:11.590
Open lab notebooks, which
is a relatively new thing,

00:04:11.590 --> 00:04:14.530
but I think an emerging
area of open science.

00:04:14.530 --> 00:04:17.950
And then some of the work
flow and repository tools

00:04:17.950 --> 00:04:21.120
that we can use
to share our work.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.550
So why open data?

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:26.550
I think most of us here are
convinced that open data is

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:28.860
a good thing, but there are
many different perspectives

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.610
we can take as to why
that's a good thing.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:32.490
So one is the
researcher perspective,

00:04:32.490 --> 00:04:34.990
why would I want to do this
from a selfish perspective?

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:39.810
Well, there are a few studies,
mainly from Heather Piwowar,

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:42.660
which show that if you
share your data associated

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:45.520
with a study, that study
then gets more citations.

00:04:45.520 --> 00:04:48.230
So this is more visibility
for the researcher

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:51.730
and a personal benefit for them.

00:04:51.730 --> 00:04:54.720
So I think it's important for
us as advocates to consider

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.240
that researchers, especially
in the early stages

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:00.930
of their career,
are understandably

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.540
interested with their
career advancement.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:05.790
We don't want anybody to
sacrifice their career

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:07.248
for the sake of
the movement, and I

00:05:07.248 --> 00:05:08.530
don't think that's necessary.

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:11.154
There are lots of different ways
that they can share their work

00:05:11.154 --> 00:05:13.500
and be successful,
and in some cases,

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:15.709
get an edge up, get
increased visibility.

00:05:15.709 --> 00:05:17.250
So that's one of
the reasons why we'd

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:21.980
like to see researchers
share, for their own benefit.

00:05:21.980 --> 00:05:24.200
Another one, so reproducibility.

00:05:24.200 --> 00:05:28.166
So I think that this
image captures nicely what

00:05:28.166 --> 00:05:30.290
we like to think about in
terms of reproducibility.

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:32.123
This is from the Wide
Open Research Project,

00:05:32.123 --> 00:05:33.890
so these cartoons are by my dad.

00:05:33.890 --> 00:05:36.470
I teamed up with
him through-- yeah,

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:39.080
it's an awesome thing to be
able to team up with a family

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:40.190
member on this project.

00:05:40.190 --> 00:05:41.810
So we teamed up,
and the idea was

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:46.970
to create very visual ways of
transmitting to researchers

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:48.620
the importance of
reproducibility,

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:51.260
the importance of open
science, and some of the ways

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:53.690
that they could benefit
from sharing their own work.

00:05:53.690 --> 00:05:56.597
So on the web
page, which I think

00:05:56.597 --> 00:05:59.180
you actually can't get into with
the current Wi-Fi connection.

00:05:59.180 --> 00:05:59.960
I couldn't access.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:03.200
But we do have an image
gallery, and all those images

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:04.190
are openly licensed.

00:06:04.190 --> 00:06:06.720
So you can reuse those
however you'd like.

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:09.560
So we talked a lot today
about the importance

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:11.120
of reproducibility.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:12.890
One of the tenants
of science is we'd

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:16.370
like to see that not
just this lab can produce

00:06:16.370 --> 00:06:19.090
this particular result. But
when I take those methods

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:20.780
and I try to do
it in my own lab,

00:06:20.780 --> 00:06:24.390
that I can get the
same or similar result,

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:27.070
thereby confirming
their findings.

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:30.140
And so how can you do this
if you can't figure out

00:06:30.140 --> 00:06:31.550
what they've done?

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:34.430
So if you can't
understand the protocol,

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:37.460
if you can't understand
what the data looks like,

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.280
how can you go about
reproducing that study?

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:42.320
The short answer
is that you can't.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:45.710
So we need open data
for reproducibility.

00:06:45.710 --> 00:06:48.230
So this has been talked
about a few times.

00:06:48.230 --> 00:06:50.880
I think, Brian, you're
in part of this cartoon.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:52.370
So this is a great
cartoon series

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:55.370
that [INAUDIBLE] did
on reproducibility,

00:06:55.370 --> 00:06:57.060
the replication crisis.

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:01.460
And so we would like to see that
when we read through a paper,

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:04.310
we check the methods, and we
try to do that in our own lab

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:07.370
that we get a similar
or the same result.

00:07:07.370 --> 00:07:10.550
But what's happening in a lot
of these large-scale replication

00:07:10.550 --> 00:07:13.040
efforts is we're seeing
that a very small percentage

00:07:13.040 --> 00:07:15.410
of those studies can
actually be reproduced.

00:07:15.410 --> 00:07:18.380
And I think a lot of us have
personal experience with this.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:20.930
As graduate students,
you enter a lab,

00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:22.244
and they say, look, very easy.

00:07:22.244 --> 00:07:24.410
In a couple of weeks you
just reproduce this result,

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:25.530
and everything will be fine.

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:27.571
And then you start with
the rest of your project.

00:07:27.571 --> 00:07:29.660
But then you spend
a year or two years

00:07:29.660 --> 00:07:33.890
or however long trying
to reproduce that result

00:07:33.890 --> 00:07:36.920
and sometimes getting
completely different results.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:38.900
And it's very frustrating,
and it wastes a lot

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:41.520
of time and effort and money.

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:48.820
So what we'd like to see is
an acceleration of science

00:07:48.820 --> 00:07:49.820
through data sharing.

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:51.820
So this is an example
of a study that we're

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:56.020
trying to validate of 50
high-profile cancer studies,

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:59.740
and what they found was
tremendous difficulty

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:02.630
in getting access to
the original study data.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:04.450
So trying to find
out what they'd done,

00:08:04.450 --> 00:08:06.533
what the data looked like,
so that they could then

00:08:06.533 --> 00:08:08.410
go and reproduce these studies.

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:11.440
And there are many times where
you contact those authors,

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:13.510
and they just don't respond.

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:19.200
And so this is slowing
down the pace of science.

00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.220
Another thing, so I
really appreciated--

00:08:22.220 --> 00:08:25.350
[INAUDIBLE], so you were talking
about the impact, the larger

00:08:25.350 --> 00:08:27.360
impact beyond just
academia, what

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.530
is the impact of sharing data?

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:32.250
So this is a new project called
the Open Data Impact Map,

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:35.280
and what they're doing is
trying to gather together

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:37.620
case studies, again,
all over the world,

00:08:37.620 --> 00:08:41.429
about particular companies,
some social justice

00:08:41.429 --> 00:08:43.876
projects that are reusing data.

00:08:43.876 --> 00:08:46.000
In a lot of these cases,
it's open government data,

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:49.320
but in some cases, it might
be, as well, research data.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.930
And they're raising
that data, but not

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:54.660
in an academic environment,
now in a social justice

00:08:54.660 --> 00:09:00.250
or civil or public health
problems type of environment.

00:09:00.250 --> 00:09:02.220
So I just pulled out
a couple of examples

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:04.840
from Mexico that I
think are interesting.

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:07.890
So this is CREA, which
is a project that

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:10.830
tries to empower
and encourage women

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:15.750
to start their own businesses
and to be independent.

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:17.430
And this is
particularly for women

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:20.340
in socially and economically
disadvantaged areas.

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:23.400
So this is a fantastic project,
and they're using open data

00:09:23.400 --> 00:09:26.970
as part of this project.

00:09:26.970 --> 00:09:28.550
Another example.

00:09:28.550 --> 00:09:35.300
This project is looking at what
types of oil and gas mineral

00:09:35.300 --> 00:09:38.510
extraction is going on in
different areas in Mexico.

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:42.140
Is that invading on or
damaging indigenous lands?

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:44.540
How is that affecting
the environment?

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:46.940
And so they're using open
data, and I think even

00:09:46.940 --> 00:09:49.850
in some cases producing
their own open data

00:09:49.850 --> 00:09:52.580
to defend these
lands and to promote

00:09:52.580 --> 00:09:55.710
human rights and environmental
conservation in these areas.

00:09:55.710 --> 00:09:58.700
So fantastic uses of open
data and just another reason

00:09:58.700 --> 00:10:03.240
why we should be caring
about this issue.

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:06.640
So but then as a researcher,
you run into this problem,

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:07.140
like I said.

00:10:07.140 --> 00:10:10.290
You say, OK, so all
this sounds great to me.

00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:12.110
I'm convinced.

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:14.637
So how do I actually
do this, right?

00:10:14.637 --> 00:10:16.220
And so I want to
emphasize this point.

00:10:16.220 --> 00:10:19.650
Dumping a spreadsheet
online is not open data.

00:10:19.650 --> 00:10:21.980
This is not what we
mean by open data,

00:10:21.980 --> 00:10:24.890
right, because dumping that
spreadsheet online doesn't

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:28.400
necessarily mean anybody else
can understand those data

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:30.320
or reuse them in any way.

00:10:30.320 --> 00:10:35.300
So how do we share our data
in a way that's understandable

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:37.640
and hopefully usable by others?

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:40.640
Because that's what
we'd like to see.

00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:43.180
So I think the first
step for me, especially,

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:46.210
was to understand what are
the international standards.

00:10:46.210 --> 00:10:47.800
What do we mean by open data?

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:50.360
If we don't mean it's just
dumping a spreadsheet,

00:10:50.360 --> 00:10:51.460
what do we mean?

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:54.850
So this is the open definition
from the Open Knowledge

00:10:54.850 --> 00:10:57.940
Foundation, and they say that
"open data and content can

00:10:57.940 --> 00:11:00.490
be freely used,
modified, and shared

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:02.420
by anyone for any purpose."

00:11:02.420 --> 00:11:05.080
So that's their definition.

00:11:05.080 --> 00:11:06.520
It sounds broad,
but I think it's

00:11:06.520 --> 00:11:10.750
important to emphasize this
issue of modify and reuse.

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:12.370
So if I dump a
spreadsheet out there

00:11:12.370 --> 00:11:15.360
that nobody else can understand,
nobody's going to modify that.

00:11:15.360 --> 00:11:16.720
Nobody's going to reuse that.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:19.310
That's not open data.

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:21.790
So this is one of the
international standards

00:11:21.790 --> 00:11:24.620
with respect to open data.

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:27.520
Another one is the
Panton Principles,

00:11:27.520 --> 00:11:30.670
and I like this because the
name comes from the fact

00:11:30.670 --> 00:11:33.670
that the first draft was put
together at the pub called

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:35.590
Panton Arms in Cambridge.

00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:38.800
And so this is the group
that worked on that.

00:11:38.800 --> 00:11:40.640
So they just have
sound very basic

00:11:40.640 --> 00:11:44.290
but, I think, important
recommendations to state.

00:11:44.290 --> 00:11:46.810
So the first is that if you're
going to share your data,

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:50.350
you should share it along with
an explicit statement on how

00:11:50.350 --> 00:11:53.770
you want those data
to be reused or what

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:56.430
reuse you want to limit.

00:11:56.430 --> 00:11:58.980
That you should use
a recognized waiver,

00:11:58.980 --> 00:12:01.360
a legal type of
waiver or license

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:07.100
that specifies the rights, the
reuse rights on those data,

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:09.282
particularly that you
should make data truly open.

00:12:09.282 --> 00:12:10.990
And that, what we mean
here is that there

00:12:10.990 --> 00:12:12.310
are no restrictive clauses.

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:15.400
So it's very common
to see data or even

00:12:15.400 --> 00:12:18.070
articles released under
a Creative Commons

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:21.370
license that has a no
commercial restriction.

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:23.770
And people, at least
intuitively, think, well yes.

00:12:23.770 --> 00:12:25.450
I'd like to limit
commercial entities

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:29.380
coming in, packaging up my
materials, and reselling it.

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:34.390
But what it does that is not an
intended restriction, I think,

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:36.760
is that there are
some cases where

00:12:36.760 --> 00:12:39.100
educational institutions
and even nonprofits

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:41.040
can be considered
commercial entities.

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:43.041
And then that restricts
their reuse of the data.

00:12:43.041 --> 00:12:44.414
So we don't want
to start putting

00:12:44.414 --> 00:12:45.790
those types of restrictions.

00:12:45.790 --> 00:12:49.030
If possible we want to place
data in the public domain

00:12:49.030 --> 00:12:51.820
so people can reuse
it, remix it however

00:12:51.820 --> 00:12:54.850
they like without
worrying about tracking

00:12:54.850 --> 00:13:00.989
the attributions or any
commercial restrictions there.

00:13:00.989 --> 00:13:02.530
And by the way, as
a point of action,

00:13:02.530 --> 00:13:04.780
you can sign on to
these Panton Principles

00:13:04.780 --> 00:13:07.510
and say that you
agree with these.

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:09.040
So step 2.

00:13:09.040 --> 00:13:11.672
You know the definition
of open data.

00:13:11.672 --> 00:13:13.630
You've decided you're
going to share your data.

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:15.430
You need to find a
trusted repository,

00:13:15.430 --> 00:13:17.260
and you have a lot of choices.

00:13:17.260 --> 00:13:20.200
This is another thing that's
overwhelming for researchers.

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:21.930
Where do I put these data?

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:23.440
So I've just put a
few options here.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:26.890
Open Science Framework that
was discussed this morning.

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:30.760
GitHub is another common one,
more commonly for sharing code,

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:32.320
but you can also share data.

00:13:32.320 --> 00:13:34.640
And you can have the data
and the code together,

00:13:34.640 --> 00:13:36.560
which can be advantageous.

00:13:36.560 --> 00:13:39.250
Data Dryad and Zenodo,
which is run by CERN.

00:13:39.250 --> 00:13:41.750
Figshare is one
that I use a lot,

00:13:41.750 --> 00:13:44.530
and the reason that I use it
is because it's very easy.

00:13:44.530 --> 00:13:45.970
It's user friendly.

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:47.979
It's fast to upload things.

00:13:47.979 --> 00:13:49.520
And this is another
important factor.

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:50.830
If we're talking to
researchers and we're

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:52.496
trying to convince
them to do something,

00:13:52.496 --> 00:13:54.130
we want that low
barrier to entry.

00:13:54.130 --> 00:13:57.490
We don't want to make their
work flow more complicated.

00:13:57.490 --> 00:13:58.722
We want to make it easier.

00:13:58.722 --> 00:14:01.180
I think this is something we
also saw with the Open Science

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:01.679
Framework.

00:14:01.679 --> 00:14:03.550
We want to make things
easier and faster.

00:14:03.550 --> 00:14:06.130
Instead of having to visit a
number of different platforms,

00:14:06.130 --> 00:14:09.140
if you have that integration,
things go much smoother.

00:14:09.140 --> 00:14:10.690
So all of these
platforms kind of

00:14:10.690 --> 00:14:13.690
have their different features,
but they're all archiving.

00:14:13.690 --> 00:14:16.660
They have some type of
preservation through locks.

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:20.800
You get, for most of these, some
type of permanent identifier

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:22.870
that you can go back to
or that people can site.

00:14:22.870 --> 00:14:24.370
There are metrics,
you know, number

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:26.830
of forks of your repository,
how many people are

00:14:26.830 --> 00:14:30.040
looking at it, how many people
are reusing and contributing

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:30.880
to it.

00:14:30.880 --> 00:14:35.810
So any of these are good
solutions for sharing data.

00:14:35.810 --> 00:14:38.867
Let's talk about formats.

00:14:38.867 --> 00:14:40.950
So you've decided you're
going to share your data.

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:42.320
You picked your repository.

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:44.570
And then what are you going
to put in that repository?

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:46.861
Again, we're not going to
dump our spreadsheets, right?

00:14:46.861 --> 00:14:49.230
And we're definitely not
going to put things in PDF.

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:51.220
So-- we'll talk about
that in a minute.

00:14:51.220 --> 00:14:53.910
So not everyone-- it's important
to recognize not everyone

00:14:53.910 --> 00:14:56.880
has a Microsoft license
at their institution.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:59.520
These licenses are expensive.

00:14:59.520 --> 00:15:02.790
And a lot of institutions in
the US and Canada and Europe

00:15:02.790 --> 00:15:04.890
may be fortunate to
have those licenses,

00:15:04.890 --> 00:15:07.200
but other institutions in
other parts of the world

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:08.650
don't have that.

00:15:08.650 --> 00:15:10.680
So if you're releasing
your file formats

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:15.390
in those proprietary formats,
they can't access those data.

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:19.380
So it may be there, but it may
still be inaccessible to them.

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:21.990
So sharing in these
nonproprietary formats

00:15:21.990 --> 00:15:24.990
allows everybody to open those
files and work with them.

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:26.700
So here we kind of have a chart.

00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:30.540
You've got your, you've got
your proprietary file formats

00:15:30.540 --> 00:15:34.860
over here, things like
Microsoft Word, Microsoft Excel,

00:15:34.860 --> 00:15:36.630
and here you have kind
of the equivalent.

00:15:36.630 --> 00:15:38.820
So instead of Excel, we
can publish those data

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:40.560
in CSV format, and
that's something

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:43.260
that most people should be
able to open even though they

00:15:43.260 --> 00:15:45.570
don't have a Microsoft license.

00:15:45.570 --> 00:15:48.520
And then we have, also, this
issue of machine readability.

00:15:48.520 --> 00:15:51.840
So a PDF is not an
ideal format for data,

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:53.987
although many governments
are using this.

00:15:53.987 --> 00:15:56.070
Mexico releases a lot of
its-- although it's great

00:15:56.070 --> 00:15:58.005
that we release the
open government data,

00:15:58.005 --> 00:16:00.310
a lot of that is in PDF format.

00:16:00.310 --> 00:16:01.770
So if you want to
use it, you have

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:04.050
to copy each row
into a spreadsheet,

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:05.280
and it takes a long time.

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:08.750
And it's just not an efficient
way of sharing those data.

00:16:08.750 --> 00:16:11.270
So thinking about
file formats is

00:16:11.270 --> 00:16:14.270
something important in
terms of accessibility,

00:16:14.270 --> 00:16:17.200
the data you're sharing.

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:21.080
So Tim Berners-Lee has developed
this great five-star rating

00:16:21.080 --> 00:16:23.520
system for open data.

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:26.370
And so, as you can see,
PDF falls on the end,

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:29.370
the one-star end of this scale.

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:33.390
And so the idea is that,
well, it's not ideal,

00:16:33.390 --> 00:16:34.859
but it's out there,
so you should

00:16:34.859 --> 00:16:36.150
at least get one star for that.

00:16:36.150 --> 00:16:38.170
OK, so you've shared it
in some type of format,

00:16:38.170 --> 00:16:39.600
but it's not machine readable.

00:16:39.600 --> 00:16:41.810
It might be in a
proprietary format,

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:44.389
and you can't really reuse
that or modify that, right?

00:16:44.389 --> 00:16:45.930
This is part of that
open definition.

00:16:45.930 --> 00:16:47.560
You can't modify it.

00:16:47.560 --> 00:16:49.510
So going up to the next level.

00:16:49.510 --> 00:16:52.620
Let's make it available
as structured data.

00:16:52.620 --> 00:16:55.620
So two stars if you've got that
in some type of spreadsheet

00:16:55.620 --> 00:16:59.430
format that now people at least
can modify, play with, assuming

00:16:59.430 --> 00:17:02.984
they have the license to
open those Excel files.

00:17:02.984 --> 00:17:04.650
Going up to the next
level, three stars.

00:17:04.650 --> 00:17:07.810
So now you've got
openly licensed data.

00:17:07.810 --> 00:17:09.339
You've got it machine readable.

00:17:09.339 --> 00:17:12.241
And now you have it in a
nonproprietary file format

00:17:12.241 --> 00:17:14.740
that people all over, even if
they don't have that Microsoft

00:17:14.740 --> 00:17:18.180
license, can open,
modify, reuse.

00:17:18.180 --> 00:17:20.260
And then you've got
these higher levels

00:17:20.260 --> 00:17:25.180
that are related to adding URLs
and then linking your data set

00:17:25.180 --> 00:17:26.516
to other data sets.

00:17:26.516 --> 00:17:28.099
So these are the
most advanced levels.

00:17:28.099 --> 00:17:30.619
My shared data,
I'm still at three.

00:17:30.619 --> 00:17:32.410
I haven't gotten to
four or five stars yet,

00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:33.326
but I think that's OK.

00:17:33.326 --> 00:17:36.000
I'm happy with three
stars, for now.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:38.140
But just to kind of
emphasize the point

00:17:38.140 --> 00:17:40.790
that not all open
data is created equal.

00:17:40.790 --> 00:17:43.240
So it depends how we're
sharing these data,

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:46.120
what format they're in, and
how we're connecting them

00:17:46.120 --> 00:17:47.860
with other data
sets that determines

00:17:47.860 --> 00:17:49.280
how reusable those data are.

00:17:52.610 --> 00:17:54.890
So we like badges, here.

00:17:54.890 --> 00:17:57.090
I think we're all agreed
that we like badges.

00:17:57.090 --> 00:17:59.580
And so you can get these badges
and stick them on your data

00:17:59.580 --> 00:18:02.130
sets and say, hey, this
is the level of compliance

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:03.799
I have based on this
five-star system.

00:18:03.799 --> 00:18:05.840
And then people also know
what they can and can't

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:07.940
do with that data, right?

00:18:07.940 --> 00:18:11.930
So again, OL meaning you have
an open license on there.

00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:14.270
Machine readable, and
then an open format.

00:18:14.270 --> 00:18:18.940
So my data is currently out
three stars out of five.

00:18:18.940 --> 00:18:22.840
So what happens if you have
data, you've collected it,

00:18:22.840 --> 00:18:25.090
and it's in a
proprietary format.

00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:30.160
And you really don't have a good
way of sharing it or converting

00:18:30.160 --> 00:18:32.530
it to another format?

00:18:32.530 --> 00:18:34.570
So I ran into this--

00:18:34.570 --> 00:18:36.580
we collect neural
recording data,

00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:40.810
and a lot of the software that
allows you to take these to--

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:43.780
register these recordings
is proprietary.

00:18:43.780 --> 00:18:46.570
And so you end up with files
that other people can't open,

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:49.810
and those licenses are
ridiculous expensive.

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:52.780
So what we did in
this case was--

00:18:52.780 --> 00:18:55.414
someone was talking about
Stack Overflow at lunch.

00:18:55.414 --> 00:18:57.580
So I want to give a big
shout out to Stack Overflow,

00:18:57.580 --> 00:19:00.460
because the fantastic thing
about this open source

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:03.700
community is you can go on there
and say, I have this file type.

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:06.520
I need to figure out how to make
it readable to other people.

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:07.550
How do I do that?

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:09.370
And there's a community of
people who already figured out

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:11.661
how to do that and are willing
to share their solutions

00:19:11.661 --> 00:19:12.190
with you.

00:19:12.190 --> 00:19:15.700
So Stack Overflow can be
this amazing tool for you

00:19:15.700 --> 00:19:18.162
to figure out how
to do these very--

00:19:18.162 --> 00:19:19.870
what sound like simple
things but may not

00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:21.450
be entirely trivial.

00:19:21.450 --> 00:19:24.040
So I wrote this small,
little piece of code,

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:26.350
and a lot of it's copy-paste
from some of the solutions

00:19:26.350 --> 00:19:27.680
that I found on Stack Overflow.

00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:29.530
That's great.

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:33.520
And that allowed me to put that
code up with my data and say,

00:19:33.520 --> 00:19:35.800
if you don't have this
particular software

00:19:35.800 --> 00:19:37.900
to open the file,
you can open it

00:19:37.900 --> 00:19:39.670
with this little piece of code.

00:19:39.670 --> 00:19:42.480
And so that solved the
proprietary file format issue.

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:45.280
So even people who didn't have
that, they can now punch--

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:45.880
and it shows--

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:48.010
I show them how to run
this little piece of code

00:19:48.010 --> 00:19:49.900
so they can open all the files.

00:19:49.900 --> 00:19:51.650
And there's no issue there.

00:19:51.650 --> 00:19:53.590
So there are
workarounds you can use,

00:19:53.590 --> 00:19:55.420
and there are these
great communities that

00:19:55.420 --> 00:19:57.980
are willing to help you figure
out how to do this stuff.

00:20:00.740 --> 00:20:05.910
So step 4, create a master data
log file, or in other words,

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:07.010
show me the metadata.

00:20:07.010 --> 00:20:11.240
So again, dumping a spreadsheet
online, not open data.

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:14.000
Because a lot of the time what
we have in those spreadsheets

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:15.770
are these abbreviations
that nobody else

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:17.360
but us can understand.

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:20.604
And so in my case, I
have-- there's one person

00:20:20.604 --> 00:20:23.020
in the audience I know who
understands these abbreviations

00:20:23.020 --> 00:20:24.895
but that's because we
worked in the same lab.

00:20:24.895 --> 00:20:28.880
So there are these
abbreviations of genetic lines

00:20:28.880 --> 00:20:31.500
that I'm using, and if I just
dump that out there, nobody

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:34.550
is going to understand what
that means or how to reuse it.

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:37.580
So I need to have some
information on that.

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:39.980
And then this file,
in particular,

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:42.530
has all the files that
I'm going to release

00:20:42.530 --> 00:20:43.650
as part of the data set.

00:20:43.650 --> 00:20:50.960
So it has here the date for
each recording, the channel

00:20:50.960 --> 00:20:53.810
or the file number that
corresponds to that

00:20:53.810 --> 00:20:56.400
file that I'm going
to share with you,

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:58.070
the particular
experimental condition.

00:20:58.070 --> 00:21:00.830
In this case, it's a
genetic manipulation.

00:21:00.830 --> 00:21:02.990
And then all the
data here, and I

00:21:02.990 --> 00:21:05.660
have to explain to you, what
does "birth start A" mean?

00:21:05.660 --> 00:21:06.740
Do you know, do you
know what that means?

00:21:06.740 --> 00:21:08.390
No, you shouldn't
know what that means.

00:21:08.390 --> 00:21:10.340
I should explain that to you.

00:21:10.340 --> 00:21:12.320
So I think it's
important to emphasize

00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:16.640
that your data are really not
as clear as you think they are.

00:21:16.640 --> 00:21:19.080
You've been working with
these data for a long time.

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:20.839
You've been looking
at them every day.

00:21:20.839 --> 00:21:22.880
They make sense to you,
but they don't make sense

00:21:22.880 --> 00:21:24.020
to other people.

00:21:24.020 --> 00:21:25.670
And so you have
to explain what's

00:21:25.670 --> 00:21:28.890
there so that
people can reuse it.

00:21:28.890 --> 00:21:31.430
So part of that is
creating what they

00:21:31.430 --> 00:21:34.910
call a README file, a detailed
README file that explains

00:21:34.910 --> 00:21:37.260
exactly what's in the data set.

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:39.350
So what is included?

00:21:39.350 --> 00:21:41.870
Does this data set correspond
to a published article

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:46.070
or pre-print, because we
want a link up those outputs.

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:47.900
That's where we're
linking open access

00:21:47.900 --> 00:21:50.840
to publications with open
data that corresponds

00:21:50.840 --> 00:21:52.540
to that publication.

00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:54.220
You have to explain
the overall data

00:21:54.220 --> 00:21:57.640
structure, any
abbreviations or terminology

00:21:57.640 --> 00:22:00.250
that you're using that might
be unusual, and explain

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:01.610
any missing data.

00:22:01.610 --> 00:22:03.190
So in my data set,
for example, I

00:22:03.190 --> 00:22:08.620
was recording a pattern of motor
activity in a little Drosophila

00:22:08.620 --> 00:22:09.790
larva.

00:22:09.790 --> 00:22:12.430
And what happens is
not every animal has

00:22:12.430 --> 00:22:14.560
the same amount of
activity, so that

00:22:14.560 --> 00:22:16.420
means if I'm recording
those bursts,

00:22:16.420 --> 00:22:19.804
not every animal in that sample
has the same amount of data.

00:22:19.804 --> 00:22:21.220
And so I need to
explain to people

00:22:21.220 --> 00:22:26.172
why it is that this recording
has 10 bursts, and this has 30.

00:22:26.172 --> 00:22:27.130
Or something like that.

00:22:27.130 --> 00:22:29.660
Why are the files sets
not the same size?

00:22:29.660 --> 00:22:32.110
So, this is just an example.

00:22:32.110 --> 00:22:35.110
I put my README file
up here on GitHub.

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:37.990
And then I linked
it, and I said,

00:22:37.990 --> 00:22:40.180
all the code and data
in this repository

00:22:40.180 --> 00:22:42.800
correspond to the pre-print
that can be found here.

00:22:42.800 --> 00:22:47.480
So I'm linking, now, my
publication with my data set.

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:48.620
So here's that publication.

00:22:48.620 --> 00:22:49.880
This is a pre-print.

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:51.380
I never succeeded
in getting this

00:22:51.380 --> 00:22:54.750
to go through official
journal review.

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:56.510
But that's OK, because
it's out there.

00:22:56.510 --> 00:22:58.430
And in particular,
this was a null result.

00:22:58.430 --> 00:23:00.680
So I did a genetic manipulation.

00:23:00.680 --> 00:23:03.837
It didn't have an effect, or
at least a measurable effect.

00:23:03.837 --> 00:23:05.420
And I think it's
important that people

00:23:05.420 --> 00:23:08.640
know that I did that study
and didn't find anything,

00:23:08.640 --> 00:23:11.060
because again, there might
be graduate students in labs

00:23:11.060 --> 00:23:12.860
somewhere in the world
trying to do this again.

00:23:12.860 --> 00:23:15.193
And they should know that
somebody has already tried it,

00:23:15.193 --> 00:23:16.490
and it didn't work, right?

00:23:16.490 --> 00:23:20.000
So I put this pre-print
up on PeerJ Preprints,

00:23:20.000 --> 00:23:22.190
which is one of the pre-print--

00:23:22.190 --> 00:23:24.140
one of the many
pre-print servers.

00:23:24.140 --> 00:23:27.980
And I put a note on
that that now says,

00:23:27.980 --> 00:23:30.590
all the data and analysis
code associated with the study

00:23:30.590 --> 00:23:32.780
can be found in this
GitHub repository.

00:23:32.780 --> 00:23:36.050
So on my repository in GitHub,
it's linked to my pre-print.

00:23:36.050 --> 00:23:39.380
On my pre-print in PeerJ,
it's linked back to my data,

00:23:39.380 --> 00:23:42.450
so that everything
is integrated.

00:23:42.450 --> 00:23:45.370
And I think this will
stay a pre-print forever,

00:23:45.370 --> 00:23:46.080
but that's OK.

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:48.671
I'm fine with that.

00:23:48.671 --> 00:23:49.170
All right.

00:23:49.170 --> 00:23:50.806
So you've done all that.

00:23:50.806 --> 00:23:51.930
You've put your data there.

00:23:51.930 --> 00:23:53.880
It's in nonproprietary
format, hopefully

00:23:53.880 --> 00:23:55.800
you've got three stars.

00:23:55.800 --> 00:23:58.170
You've got your
README file there.

00:23:58.170 --> 00:24:00.420
And then what you need
to explain to people

00:24:00.420 --> 00:24:03.720
is at least how you analyzed it.

00:24:03.720 --> 00:24:06.750
So they may come up with
another way to analyze it,

00:24:06.750 --> 00:24:09.150
but you should have
some explanation,

00:24:09.150 --> 00:24:11.220
especially if you've
developed special code.

00:24:11.220 --> 00:24:14.550
So with this project, I
developed a Python code

00:24:14.550 --> 00:24:18.850
that was specific to
analyzing these type of data,

00:24:18.850 --> 00:24:21.030
and so I should
explain to people

00:24:21.030 --> 00:24:22.830
what I did with that code.

00:24:22.830 --> 00:24:26.670
So one of the great tools that's
developed in the last few years

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:28.380
are these electronic notebooks.

00:24:28.380 --> 00:24:30.010
So I-- they used to
be called IPython.

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:33.250
Now they're Jupyter Notebooks,
but it's the same idea.

00:24:33.250 --> 00:24:36.780
So this is an open source,
very interactive platform

00:24:36.780 --> 00:24:42.000
for sharing code and hopefully
sharing a detailed explanation

00:24:42.000 --> 00:24:43.816
that walks people
through the code

00:24:43.816 --> 00:24:45.690
and allows them to
understand exactly what it

00:24:45.690 --> 00:24:47.130
does in each part.

00:24:47.130 --> 00:24:50.890
So let me see if I can
show you an example here.

00:24:50.890 --> 00:24:54.200
So what I first needed
to explain to people

00:24:54.200 --> 00:24:58.460
was how to access the data,
how to open the files.

00:24:58.460 --> 00:25:00.590
Sounds trivial, but it
wasn't in this case.

00:25:00.590 --> 00:25:03.571
That's why I had that
little piece of code that

00:25:03.571 --> 00:25:04.820
allowed them to open the file.

00:25:04.820 --> 00:25:06.800
So I needed to
explain to them, OK.

00:25:06.800 --> 00:25:08.912
So first of all, you
need to open the file.

00:25:08.912 --> 00:25:10.370
Here's how you're
going to open it,

00:25:10.370 --> 00:25:12.206
and here's how you're
going to plot it.

00:25:12.206 --> 00:25:13.580
And so that's what
this explains.

00:25:13.580 --> 00:25:15.890
So I put my little command here.

00:25:15.890 --> 00:25:18.440
And that's an interactive
cell, and you just

00:25:18.440 --> 00:25:20.270
press, you run the code
that's in the cell.

00:25:20.270 --> 00:25:24.900
And then the output
comes out here below.

00:25:24.900 --> 00:25:27.820
So here I've said, well, let's
read this particular file

00:25:27.820 --> 00:25:29.640
that's in the data set.

00:25:29.640 --> 00:25:32.091
I want to read it
within this time window.

00:25:32.091 --> 00:25:33.590
And they can change
that time window

00:25:33.590 --> 00:25:36.410
if they'd like to view a
different part of the data set.

00:25:36.410 --> 00:25:38.690
And then when I run
that cell, here's

00:25:38.690 --> 00:25:41.480
what it's going to
produce as an output.

00:25:41.480 --> 00:25:43.580
And the nice thing is,
because it's interactive,

00:25:43.580 --> 00:25:45.020
they can change it.

00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:47.630
So they could change
this file number.

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:49.290
They can view a
different one, or they

00:25:49.290 --> 00:25:50.540
can change the window of time.

00:25:50.540 --> 00:25:52.757
If they want to zoom in
on one of these bursts,

00:25:52.757 --> 00:25:54.340
it's not static in
other words, right?

00:25:54.340 --> 00:25:57.890
So they can go and they can
interact with those data

00:25:57.890 --> 00:25:58.892
however they'd like.

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:01.700
All right.

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:04.010
So they can open the
files, and now I'm

00:26:04.010 --> 00:26:06.500
explaining to them
with a notebook how

00:26:06.500 --> 00:26:08.090
I analyzed the data.

00:26:08.090 --> 00:26:10.580
So telling them, run
this little piece of code

00:26:10.580 --> 00:26:13.520
that I have, specify
the CSV files,

00:26:13.520 --> 00:26:16.070
because we have a
nonproprietary file format.

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:18.630
So run these files.

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:22.020
And I'm explaining to them here
that you're running a control

00:26:22.020 --> 00:26:24.750
file, and you're
comparing that to one

00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:26.820
of the genetic manipulations.

00:26:26.820 --> 00:26:27.990
And then I'm saying, OK.

00:26:27.990 --> 00:26:30.480
You go through, you run
this, and then-- poof--

00:26:30.480 --> 00:26:33.090
you get all your
data right here.

00:26:33.090 --> 00:26:35.817
And they can-- again, they
can change the spreadsheet

00:26:35.817 --> 00:26:36.900
that they want to look at.

00:26:36.900 --> 00:26:38.070
They can change the data set.

00:26:38.070 --> 00:26:39.660
So if they think,
for example, I might

00:26:39.660 --> 00:26:43.470
have picked a very neat,
clean pretty data set,

00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:45.270
then they can change
this and look and see

00:26:45.270 --> 00:26:47.310
if the other sets
are that pretty.

00:26:47.310 --> 00:26:50.040
Or if I'm presenting one
that's not representative.

00:26:50.040 --> 00:26:53.190
So again, increasing
transparency by letting them

00:26:53.190 --> 00:26:56.990
interact with the data and plot
whatever they'd like to plot.

00:26:56.990 --> 00:27:01.630
So it spits out the data,
and then, of course,

00:27:01.630 --> 00:27:03.940
you'd like them to be
able to visualize it.

00:27:03.940 --> 00:27:07.360
So in this, I'm
explaining to them--

00:27:07.360 --> 00:27:09.700
there's obviously more
explanation involved here--

00:27:09.700 --> 00:27:11.500
but explaining to
them, OK, so here's

00:27:11.500 --> 00:27:12.970
how we're going
to plot the data.

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:14.860
And here's how we're
going to compare the two

00:27:14.860 --> 00:27:17.605
conditions, the control in black
and the genetic manipulation

00:27:17.605 --> 00:27:18.280
in white.

00:27:18.280 --> 00:27:20.045
We're going to overlay
those distributions

00:27:20.045 --> 00:27:22.420
and see if there's any change
between the two conditions.

00:27:22.420 --> 00:27:24.211
This is where you can
see it has no effect.

00:27:24.211 --> 00:27:27.340
Those distributions were
almost completely overlapping.

00:27:27.340 --> 00:27:29.320
But the great
thing here is these

00:27:29.320 --> 00:27:32.170
are the same figures that
are included in my pre-print.

00:27:32.170 --> 00:27:34.810
So if they'd like to
check exactly what I did,

00:27:34.810 --> 00:27:37.900
exactly how I created those
figures, they can go back

00:27:37.900 --> 00:27:40.570
and run the same
analyzes and see

00:27:40.570 --> 00:27:44.260
if it spits out the same
figures that I claim

00:27:44.260 --> 00:27:47.830
are the data in the pre-print.

00:27:47.830 --> 00:27:50.860
So I think the
message here is really

00:27:50.860 --> 00:27:56.900
that open data is
reproducible science.

00:27:56.900 --> 00:27:58.895
Open science is
reproducible science.

00:27:58.895 --> 00:28:02.705
Got these little-- so this is
GitHub, right, the octocat.

00:28:02.705 --> 00:28:04.580
I'm not sure how they
came up with that logo.

00:28:04.580 --> 00:28:06.680
I'd love to know the
story behind that one.

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:07.580
And then Jupyter.

00:28:07.580 --> 00:28:10.400
So by combining my
GitHub repo that

00:28:10.400 --> 00:28:14.390
has all my code, all my
data, and all my explanation

00:28:14.390 --> 00:28:17.720
files, my metadata,
my READMEs, I

00:28:17.720 --> 00:28:21.380
can then have my science
be much more reproducible.

00:28:21.380 --> 00:28:23.390
Because anybody can
go in, they can access

00:28:23.390 --> 00:28:25.100
the raw and the process data.

00:28:25.100 --> 00:28:26.600
All of that's there.

00:28:26.600 --> 00:28:29.960
They can reproduce all the
analysis that in my pre-print

00:28:29.960 --> 00:28:31.630
I said I did.

00:28:31.630 --> 00:28:34.260
They can replot every
figure from the pre-print

00:28:34.260 --> 00:28:36.060
and make sure that
those comparisons are

00:28:36.060 --> 00:28:37.640
what I said they are.

00:28:37.640 --> 00:28:41.130
And they can then reanalyze my
data using different methods.

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:45.140
So let's say they decide that
the way that I was analyzing it

00:28:45.140 --> 00:28:47.075
wasn't necessarily the best way.

00:28:47.075 --> 00:28:48.950
And they've developed
another way to do that,

00:28:48.950 --> 00:28:51.080
or they've even written
a piece of code.

00:28:51.080 --> 00:28:54.530
If I have it in GutHib,
they can fork my repository.

00:28:54.530 --> 00:28:56.670
They can make their own copy.

00:28:56.670 --> 00:28:58.620
They can write
another piece of code.

00:28:58.620 --> 00:29:00.720
They can reanalyze the
data, and then they

00:29:00.720 --> 00:29:03.300
can send me a request
to incorporate that

00:29:03.300 --> 00:29:05.310
into my own project.

00:29:05.310 --> 00:29:07.830
And that way I can have people
contributing and saying,

00:29:07.830 --> 00:29:08.580
you know what?

00:29:08.580 --> 00:29:11.160
I think you didn't quite
analyze it the right way.

00:29:11.160 --> 00:29:12.810
Or hey, I analyzed
it a different way.

00:29:12.810 --> 00:29:14.370
The results came out the same.

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:17.760
And people can then feed
back into this project,

00:29:17.760 --> 00:29:19.960
contribute new tools
and an analysis.

00:29:23.540 --> 00:29:26.170
So this is by Lorena Barba.

00:29:26.170 --> 00:29:29.020
She has this great thing
called the Reproducibility PI

00:29:29.020 --> 00:29:33.640
Manifesto, and it's
really these basic things

00:29:33.640 --> 00:29:36.790
that she does, kind of
bringing this all together

00:29:36.790 --> 00:29:41.110
to make sure that her lab is
producing reproducible science.

00:29:41.110 --> 00:29:43.900
So first of all, they
have version control.

00:29:43.900 --> 00:29:46.990
So all of their code is in a
repository where every time you

00:29:46.990 --> 00:29:49.870
make a change, every
time you save a new file,

00:29:49.870 --> 00:29:50.871
that change is recorded.

00:29:50.871 --> 00:29:52.786
You can go back and look
at the whole history.

00:29:52.786 --> 00:29:54.310
You can look at
who's contributing.

00:29:54.310 --> 00:29:58.420
You don't have final, final,
not this one, that one, final,

00:29:58.420 --> 00:30:01.560
so that type of
problem is avoided.

00:30:01.560 --> 00:30:04.970
They do verification and
validation on all their code,

00:30:04.970 --> 00:30:08.090
and then they publish
those validation reports,

00:30:08.090 --> 00:30:09.680
which I think is fantastic.

00:30:09.680 --> 00:30:12.260
They openly license
all their materials.

00:30:12.260 --> 00:30:14.300
One of the tricks
she uses is, say

00:30:14.300 --> 00:30:17.690
they have figures from-- bless
you-- figures from a study.

00:30:17.690 --> 00:30:21.110
They post those on figshare
under an open license,

00:30:21.110 --> 00:30:24.010
and then no matter where
they publish afterwards,

00:30:24.010 --> 00:30:27.020
say they publish in a
subscription journal,

00:30:27.020 --> 00:30:29.660
that Creative Commons
license is irrevocable.

00:30:29.660 --> 00:30:32.570
And so those figures
are open, and anyone

00:30:32.570 --> 00:30:34.220
can go back and reuse
them, regardless

00:30:34.220 --> 00:30:37.050
of where the final
article gets published.

00:30:37.050 --> 00:30:39.200
So all of that is
openly licensed.

00:30:39.200 --> 00:30:40.107
They post pre-prints.

00:30:40.107 --> 00:30:41.690
I just have the
example of arXiv here,

00:30:41.690 --> 00:30:44.330
but again, we saw there's many
places where you can publish

00:30:44.330 --> 00:30:47.990
that work prior to
official publication,

00:30:47.990 --> 00:30:51.757
and you can even get
feedback during this process.

00:30:51.757 --> 00:30:53.340
And I think there
are some people that

00:30:53.340 --> 00:30:54.340
are very afraid of this.

00:30:54.340 --> 00:30:57.300
Why on earth would I put myself
out there and post something

00:30:57.300 --> 00:30:59.040
before it's really finished?

00:30:59.040 --> 00:31:02.700
My perspective on this is
that I'd rather somebody

00:31:02.700 --> 00:31:04.600
come to me in this
stage and say,

00:31:04.600 --> 00:31:07.560
I found an error in your
paper, than come to me

00:31:07.560 --> 00:31:09.750
when I have the published
version of record.

00:31:09.750 --> 00:31:12.540
And now I have to go
and talk to the journal

00:31:12.540 --> 00:31:15.600
and put an official
error statement

00:31:15.600 --> 00:31:17.070
or something on that article.

00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:22.070
I'd much rather that come
sooner rather than later.

00:31:22.070 --> 00:31:23.900
Open code, so they
share all their code

00:31:23.900 --> 00:31:26.360
openly under an open license.

00:31:26.360 --> 00:31:28.080
And then they have
this web presence,

00:31:28.080 --> 00:31:30.830
the idea being that they should
have a website where they're

00:31:30.830 --> 00:31:33.560
sharing these materials and
where people can contact them

00:31:33.560 --> 00:31:34.822
with feedback.

00:31:34.822 --> 00:31:36.530
One of the most
important things she says

00:31:36.530 --> 00:31:40.550
is she teaches all her students
to do science this way.

00:31:40.550 --> 00:31:44.420
And the fantastic thing about
that is, hopefully, we're

00:31:44.420 --> 00:31:46.970
creating this new
generation of scientists

00:31:46.970 --> 00:31:49.225
that academically grow
up thinking this is just

00:31:49.225 --> 00:31:50.100
the way we do things.

00:31:50.100 --> 00:31:52.820
This is part of the
normal process of science.

00:31:52.820 --> 00:31:55.120
And by the time they get
into the faculty positions,

00:31:55.120 --> 00:31:58.220
this hopefully will
start to become the norm,

00:31:58.220 --> 00:31:59.480
because they grew up that way.

00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:06.270
Open notebooks, so this is
sort of a newer development.

00:32:06.270 --> 00:32:08.300
So the idea being you're
not just sharing data,

00:32:08.300 --> 00:32:10.110
but you're sharing
this whole process.

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:12.040
And you're doing
it in real time.

00:32:12.040 --> 00:32:14.394
So all those
notebooks that you're

00:32:14.394 --> 00:32:16.560
writing in the lab what you
did with that experiment

00:32:16.560 --> 00:32:18.270
for that particular
day that used

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:21.510
to then sit there on a shelf
and gather layers of dust.

00:32:21.510 --> 00:32:23.470
And once that grad
school left the lab--

00:32:23.470 --> 00:32:26.500
that that grad student left lab,
they never got looked at again.

00:32:26.500 --> 00:32:29.160
And hopefully, we can
open up that process,

00:32:29.160 --> 00:32:32.340
make it more transparent,
and publish these somewhere.

00:32:32.340 --> 00:32:35.490
So this is an open
source malaria project.

00:32:35.490 --> 00:32:39.150
They've really been kind of
pioneers in this open notebook

00:32:39.150 --> 00:32:40.230
space.

00:32:40.230 --> 00:32:44.260
And the idea here
is that they share--

00:32:44.260 --> 00:32:47.520
see I knew my timer would
stop and just fail me--

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:49.840
that they share all their
science in real time.

00:32:49.840 --> 00:32:53.510
And they operate
under what they call

00:32:53.510 --> 00:32:54.980
the six laws of open research.

00:32:54.980 --> 00:32:56.630
So all data are open.

00:32:56.630 --> 00:32:58.340
All ideas are shared.

00:32:58.340 --> 00:33:01.400
Anyone can take part, so
kind of increasing the equity

00:33:01.400 --> 00:33:04.310
that we'd like to see in
the scientific process.

00:33:04.310 --> 00:33:06.010
No patents.

00:33:06.010 --> 00:33:08.420
They are looking for drugs
that could potentially

00:33:08.420 --> 00:33:11.630
cure or slow down malaria,
and they have committed

00:33:11.630 --> 00:33:13.980
to not patenting any of that.

00:33:13.980 --> 00:33:15.830
And I think that's amazing.

00:33:15.830 --> 00:33:17.882
Suggestions being the
best form of criticism.

00:33:17.882 --> 00:33:18.590
This one's great.

00:33:18.590 --> 00:33:22.130
Public discussion is much more
valuable than private email.

00:33:22.130 --> 00:33:24.380
And also that an open project
is bigger and not owned

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:25.580
by any particular lab.

00:33:25.580 --> 00:33:26.870
So we have a goal here.

00:33:26.870 --> 00:33:28.850
We'd like to help
people that are

00:33:28.850 --> 00:33:31.820
in countries that are suffering
from high rates of malaria.

00:33:31.820 --> 00:33:35.000
That has nothing to do
with my personal success

00:33:35.000 --> 00:33:35.780
as a scientist.

00:33:35.780 --> 00:33:38.097
That's a global
public health problem,

00:33:38.097 --> 00:33:40.430
and that's where I should be
focusing my efforts, rather

00:33:40.430 --> 00:33:43.580
on what's mine, mine, mine.

00:33:43.580 --> 00:33:47.660
So after I went through this
whole process of sharing

00:33:47.660 --> 00:33:51.872
my data, and I announced on
Twitter, look you want my code?

00:33:51.872 --> 00:33:52.580
You want my data?

00:33:52.580 --> 00:33:53.690
You can have it.

00:33:53.690 --> 00:33:55.120
It's all here.

00:33:55.120 --> 00:33:58.220
I got this tweet and it
said, "Aren't you worried

00:33:58.220 --> 00:34:00.680
someone will find something
in your data that you missed,

00:34:00.680 --> 00:34:02.180
and then they get the glory?"

00:34:02.180 --> 00:34:04.790
And I think this is
a really common fear,

00:34:04.790 --> 00:34:06.350
the fear of scooping
is something

00:34:06.350 --> 00:34:08.120
that's preventing a
lot of researchers

00:34:08.120 --> 00:34:10.670
from going and sharing
their material.

00:34:10.670 --> 00:34:13.670
And I did not have the
perfect response to this.

00:34:13.670 --> 00:34:17.510
Rik Smith-Unna did, and he
said, "If Erin missed it

00:34:17.510 --> 00:34:19.880
and nobody else
ever saw the data,

00:34:19.880 --> 00:34:21.500
the world missed
out on the knowledge

00:34:21.500 --> 00:34:23.170
and nobody got the glory."

00:34:23.170 --> 00:34:26.630
And I think that's a
perfect way of summarizing

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:27.489
the benefits here.

00:34:27.489 --> 00:34:29.600
I had been looking at
those data for a long time.

00:34:29.600 --> 00:34:33.040
If I hadn't seen it out by
then, I wasn't going to see it.

00:34:33.040 --> 00:34:35.739
And just to kind of
further drive that home

00:34:35.739 --> 00:34:39.000
and to close with two
of my favorite quotes.

00:34:39.000 --> 00:34:41.469
So from Peter Murray-Rust
and the ContentMine,

00:34:41.469 --> 00:34:45.639
"You cannot assume how others
will want to reuse your work."

00:34:45.639 --> 00:34:48.507
There may be all these
things to do with those data

00:34:48.507 --> 00:34:49.840
that you never, ever thought of.

00:34:49.840 --> 00:34:52.750
And if you don't share those,
you'll never find that out.

00:34:52.750 --> 00:34:56.139
And then by Rufus Pollock from
Open Knowledge, "The best thing

00:34:56.139 --> 00:34:58.790
to do with your data will be
thought of by somebody else."

00:34:58.790 --> 00:35:02.047
So with that, I'll close,
and open up to questions.

00:35:02.047 --> 00:35:02.880
Thank you very much.

00:35:02.880 --> 00:35:10.880
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:10.880 --> 00:35:14.380
[INAUDIBLE]

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:16.020
Use my data?

00:35:16.020 --> 00:35:18.040
Not Not very many, actually.

00:35:18.040 --> 00:35:21.400
So the repository has a few
forks there from people,

00:35:21.400 --> 00:35:22.476
mostly that I know.

00:35:22.476 --> 00:35:24.100
One of them was my
brother, who decided

00:35:24.100 --> 00:35:25.320
he wanted to improve my code.

00:35:25.320 --> 00:35:26.470
[LAUGHTER]

00:35:26.470 --> 00:35:28.434
So, he's a programmer.

00:35:28.434 --> 00:35:30.100
So not many people
have used those data.

00:35:30.100 --> 00:35:33.340
It's a very kind of
obscure data set.

00:35:33.340 --> 00:35:37.690
So I was looking at a very
specific, tiny little mutation

00:35:37.690 --> 00:35:40.040
in a Drosophila larva, and
how many people in the world

00:35:40.040 --> 00:35:41.290
are really interested in that?

00:35:41.290 --> 00:35:43.240
I don't know.

00:35:43.240 --> 00:35:46.120
But I hope someday, somebody
will find it useful.

00:35:46.120 --> 00:35:49.990
And for me, I think it was very
useful, just the whole process

00:35:49.990 --> 00:35:52.120
of figuring out how to do that.

00:35:52.120 --> 00:35:55.360
So that when, hopefully, I do
have a potentially more useful

00:35:55.360 --> 00:35:58.290
data set, that process will
go a lot faster for me.

00:36:04.340 --> 00:36:09.830
In what ways or how
does your choice

00:36:09.830 --> 00:36:13.160
to follow these principles
limit your ability

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:17.510
to make use of commercial codes
that may offer capabilities

00:36:17.510 --> 00:36:21.770
for analysis of data that
are very hard to reproduce

00:36:21.770 --> 00:36:26.580
in open source applications?

00:36:26.580 --> 00:36:28.830
So, yeah, so in my lab--

00:36:28.830 --> 00:36:31.440
I mean everything we
do, everything we use

00:36:31.440 --> 00:36:32.610
is open source.

00:36:32.610 --> 00:36:34.700
Up until recently I was
running a Linux machine.

00:36:34.700 --> 00:36:37.790
I've now converted to Mac.

00:36:37.790 --> 00:36:40.110
But, so everything
we do is open source,

00:36:40.110 --> 00:36:42.810
and I haven't run
into any limits.

00:36:42.810 --> 00:36:45.030
The most serious one was
when I was figuring out

00:36:45.030 --> 00:36:45.780
how to reopen--

00:36:45.780 --> 00:36:47.910
how to open these
recordings that I had.

00:36:47.910 --> 00:36:51.930
So that kind area of
software is very locked down

00:36:51.930 --> 00:36:54.874
by proprietary programs,
but then, you know,

00:36:54.874 --> 00:36:56.040
I went on to Stack Overflow.

00:36:56.040 --> 00:36:56.695
I found this.

00:36:56.695 --> 00:36:57.570
I solved the problem.

00:36:57.570 --> 00:36:58.740
It didn't take that long.

00:36:58.740 --> 00:37:02.660
I really haven't found that
it's been restrictive for us

00:37:02.660 --> 00:37:04.034
as researchers.

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:09.010
Let me just follow up.

00:37:09.010 --> 00:37:11.660
We have many chemists
at Yale, for instance,

00:37:11.660 --> 00:37:14.260
who make use of our high
performance computing

00:37:14.260 --> 00:37:17.470
facilities, and
they probably use

00:37:17.470 --> 00:37:18.970
half a dozen
different codes that

00:37:18.970 --> 00:37:22.940
do have an issue, quantum
chemistry, for instance.

00:37:22.940 --> 00:37:26.530
And I think that they would
argue that each code is

00:37:26.530 --> 00:37:29.890
unique in its
capabilities in some way,

00:37:29.890 --> 00:37:35.410
and I suspect that they would
think that they would produce

00:37:35.410 --> 00:37:37.540
less good science
were they restricted

00:37:37.540 --> 00:37:41.020
to use just one or two
of them that may be

00:37:41.020 --> 00:37:43.090
public domain or open source.

00:37:43.090 --> 00:37:47.590
As opposed to having a suite
of things, some of which

00:37:47.590 --> 00:37:49.570
may work better for what
they are particularly

00:37:49.570 --> 00:37:51.110
focused on than others.

00:37:51.110 --> 00:37:54.280
Is that-- so in general
terms, would that

00:37:54.280 --> 00:37:57.272
be a problem for
this kind of thing?

00:37:57.272 --> 00:37:59.950
Yeah, so I can't speak
specifically to those.

00:37:59.950 --> 00:38:02.320
I don't know those,
but I can imagine cases

00:38:02.320 --> 00:38:04.690
where that might be true.

00:38:04.690 --> 00:38:08.459
Also, there's also the problem
of reinventing the wheel.

00:38:08.459 --> 00:38:10.000
If you know that
this package already

00:38:10.000 --> 00:38:11.410
exists and does what
you want, are you

00:38:11.410 --> 00:38:13.743
really going to invest the
time to create an open source

00:38:13.743 --> 00:38:14.570
version of that?

00:38:14.570 --> 00:38:17.600
And who is going to compensate
you for that time investment?

00:38:17.600 --> 00:38:19.250
I understand that
that's an issue.

00:38:19.250 --> 00:38:22.990
So there may be moments where
it's more advantageous for you

00:38:22.990 --> 00:38:26.680
to use, at least temporarily
use, the closed solution

00:38:26.680 --> 00:38:28.470
that's there.

00:38:28.470 --> 00:38:30.430
You know, these are
also my personal choices

00:38:30.430 --> 00:38:35.660
as to the restrictions
that I put on the way

00:38:35.660 --> 00:38:38.120
we do things in the lab, but
I don't mean by any sense

00:38:38.120 --> 00:38:40.250
that everybody has
to do all or nothing.

00:38:40.250 --> 00:38:42.330
There is this continuum
of open practices,

00:38:42.330 --> 00:38:44.163
and I think it's very
important to recognize

00:38:44.163 --> 00:38:47.240
that people should do
the level of sharing

00:38:47.240 --> 00:38:48.540
that they're comfortable with.

00:38:56.230 --> 00:38:57.730
Do you have any
advice for people

00:38:57.730 --> 00:39:01.212
who have data that they
can't actually release?

00:39:01.212 --> 00:39:02.920
They want to do as
much open as they can,

00:39:02.920 --> 00:39:07.090
but maybe they have private
data that they're not

00:39:07.090 --> 00:39:08.290
allowed to release.

00:39:08.290 --> 00:39:10.640
Yeah, so I mean, there
will be data sets

00:39:10.640 --> 00:39:14.030
that for privacy concerns
or for legal reasons,

00:39:14.030 --> 00:39:15.200
you're not able to release.

00:39:15.200 --> 00:39:16.470
And that's OK.

00:39:16.470 --> 00:39:18.200
I would say the
first piece of advice

00:39:18.200 --> 00:39:21.270
is to make sure that
that's actually the case.

00:39:21.270 --> 00:39:25.820
So sometimes we tend to think
that we don't have the right

00:39:25.820 --> 00:39:28.040
to share those data.

00:39:28.040 --> 00:39:30.530
Data ownership is a
little bit tricky.

00:39:30.530 --> 00:39:33.530
Or we think that maybe
anonymizing those data

00:39:33.530 --> 00:39:35.030
might be impossible
when there might

00:39:35.030 --> 00:39:37.571
be somebody else who's figured
out how to solve that problem.

00:39:37.571 --> 00:39:39.830
So I would kind of reach
out to people in your field.

00:39:39.830 --> 00:39:42.530
Maybe reach out to even legal
in your university and ask them

00:39:42.530 --> 00:39:45.050
questions about whether--

00:39:45.050 --> 00:39:47.090
how restricted you actually are.

00:39:47.090 --> 00:39:49.534
And then, if you really
can't share the data,

00:39:49.534 --> 00:39:50.450
that's understandable.

00:39:50.450 --> 00:39:52.310
That happens sometimes,
but you can share

00:39:52.310 --> 00:39:53.810
other parts of the process.

00:39:53.810 --> 00:39:58.460
So you can share the instruments
or your analysis code or maybe

00:39:58.460 --> 00:40:00.940
some metadata, depending
on whether there's

00:40:00.940 --> 00:40:02.550
sensitive information there.

00:40:02.550 --> 00:40:05.660
So sharing all the
things that are related

00:40:05.660 --> 00:40:09.680
to that project that aren't
necessarily the data, I think,

00:40:09.680 --> 00:40:11.780
is still a great step
in the right direction.

00:40:11.780 --> 00:40:14.540
If we got all researchers
to do that, we'd be--

00:40:14.540 --> 00:40:16.030
I don't know if
we'd be here today.

00:40:16.030 --> 00:40:17.790
We'd be way better
off than we are today.

00:40:17.790 --> 00:40:21.470
So yeah, I think,
again, we don't

00:40:21.470 --> 00:40:23.450
want to tell people
it's all or nothing.

00:40:23.450 --> 00:40:24.920
You share what you can.

00:40:24.920 --> 00:40:27.560
And if there's stuff that
you can't or you're not yet

00:40:27.560 --> 00:40:30.060
comfortable with sharing,
I think that's OK.

00:40:30.060 --> 00:40:34.412
And I think we should support
people in those decisions.

00:40:34.412 --> 00:40:35.350
Hi.

00:40:35.350 --> 00:40:38.650
So regarding the malaria
project that you talked about,

00:40:38.650 --> 00:40:42.700
so they kind of pledged
to do no patents,

00:40:42.700 --> 00:40:46.270
but that doesn't start--
or stop the other companies

00:40:46.270 --> 00:40:47.470
on getting patents, right?

00:40:47.470 --> 00:40:50.290
So I mean, there is actually
a lot of debate in genomics--

00:40:50.290 --> 00:40:53.740
I don't know if you're
aware regarding this stuff.

00:40:53.740 --> 00:40:56.290
So I mean, do you
have any ideas on how

00:40:56.290 --> 00:41:01.502
to do open science with this
threat of the companies--

00:41:01.502 --> 00:41:03.460
well, I mean for example,
like, human genome is

00:41:03.460 --> 00:41:08.539
kind of protected by, right
now, the judges, I guess.

00:41:08.539 --> 00:41:09.330
So to speak, right?

00:41:09.330 --> 00:41:11.350
They cannot really
patent human--

00:41:11.350 --> 00:41:13.942
like parts of human genome, no.

00:41:13.942 --> 00:41:15.650
Otherwise, we would
be like-- we wouldn't

00:41:15.650 --> 00:41:17.430
be able to do any
good science, right?

00:41:17.430 --> 00:41:19.400
So I mean what are
your thoughts on this,

00:41:19.400 --> 00:41:22.940
like how to do science with--

00:41:22.940 --> 00:41:25.880
Yeah, so I'll preface this by
saying that I'm not a lawyer,

00:41:25.880 --> 00:41:28.670
so let's get that
disclaimer out of the way.

00:41:28.670 --> 00:41:30.890
So in the case of the open
source malaria project,

00:41:30.890 --> 00:41:35.720
they are posting their
progress on a daily basis.

00:41:35.720 --> 00:41:38.840
They're publicly sharing
their entire process and all

00:41:38.840 --> 00:41:41.660
the data, which means that,
as far as I understood,

00:41:41.660 --> 00:41:44.130
that that would
count as prior art.

00:41:44.130 --> 00:41:48.770
So if somebody else tried to
file a patent based on that,

00:41:48.770 --> 00:41:51.350
they could very-- they have
all the date and time stamps.

00:41:51.350 --> 00:41:53.460
They could very easily say, no.

00:41:53.460 --> 00:41:55.490
We have already done a
public disclosure of this

00:41:55.490 --> 00:41:56.600
that counts as prior art.

00:41:56.600 --> 00:41:59.030
You cannot claim
a patent on that.

00:41:59.030 --> 00:42:01.204
That's what I understand,
at least under US law.

00:42:01.204 --> 00:42:02.870
Patent law varies a
lot internationally,

00:42:02.870 --> 00:42:05.685
so there might be some
exceptions to that.

00:42:05.685 --> 00:42:07.310
But there shouldn't
be the case that it

00:42:07.310 --> 00:42:10.850
would be very easy for someone
to just come in and co-opt all

00:42:10.850 --> 00:42:13.380
their material and patent that.

00:42:13.380 --> 00:42:16.190
I do get a lot of questions
about whether commercialization

00:42:16.190 --> 00:42:19.741
is necessarily mutually
exclusive with open science.

00:42:19.741 --> 00:42:20.990
I don't think that's the case.

00:42:20.990 --> 00:42:23.220
There are some great
models in open source--

00:42:23.220 --> 00:42:25.850
so Mozilla being one-- that
they share all their code,

00:42:25.850 --> 00:42:28.916
and they still manage to have
a successful business model.

00:42:28.916 --> 00:42:30.290
There are companies
that do that.

00:42:30.290 --> 00:42:33.350
So they release the code,
but what they commercialize

00:42:33.350 --> 00:42:36.740
is their personal
one-on-one advice

00:42:36.740 --> 00:42:39.290
about how to run the code
or troubleshoot or whatever.

00:42:39.290 --> 00:42:41.920
And they still manage to
run successful companies,

00:42:41.920 --> 00:42:45.260
commercializing that aspect
while making all the code free.

00:42:45.260 --> 00:42:47.300
So I think there
are lots of ways,

00:42:47.300 --> 00:42:51.300
creative ways that
companies can exist,

00:42:51.300 --> 00:42:54.040
that research products can
be commercialized and still

00:42:54.040 --> 00:42:57.850
be open.

00:42:57.850 --> 00:43:00.247
Any more questions?

00:43:00.247 --> 00:43:01.440
No?

00:43:01.440 --> 00:43:01.980
All right.

00:43:01.980 --> 00:43:04.370
Thank you very much.

